## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of prefix-based searching using a practical, lightweight data structure. It combines Trie fundamentals with per-node augmentation to enable fast prefix queries in real time—useful in autocomplete systems, IP routing, and spell checking.

## Key Concepts
- **Trie structure**: Each node contains 26 children (one per letter) and maintains a pass counter representing how many words pass through that node
- **insert operation**: O(m) time, where m is word length; creates nodes as needed and increments pass at each node visited
- **count_with_prefix operation**: O(k) time, where k is prefix length; traverses to the prefix node and returns its pass counter
- **Handling duplicates**: Pass counters naturally accumulate, so duplicate insertions simply increment the counter without special handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.pass = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) node.next[idx] = new TrieNode();
      node.pass++;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    node.pass++;
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) return 0;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    return node.pass;
  }
}

// Example usage
const trie = new Trie();
['cat', 'car', 'cart', 'dog', 'dot'].forEach(w => trie.insert(w));
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('ca')); // 3
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('car')); // 1
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('d')); // 2
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('do')); // 2
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-key streaming stats with independent sliding windows, combining deques, hashtables, and a heap with lazy expirations. It mirrors real services that analyze per-entity signals under time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key deques for time-bounded data
- Hash map for key -> stats
- Max-heap with lazy deletion to keep top averages up-to-date
- Expiration handling driven by currentTime

## Code Example
```javascript
class PerKeyRollingStats {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.map = new Map(); this.heap = new MaxHeap(); }
  add(key, value, ts) {
    let rec = this.map.get(key) || { dq: [], sum: 0 };
    rec.dq.push([ts, value]);
    rec.sum += value;
    while (rec.dq.length && rec.dq[0][0] < ts - this.W) {
      const [t, v] = rec.dq.shift();
      rec.sum -= v;
    }
    this.map.set(key, rec);
    const avg = rec.sum / rec.dq.length;
    this.heap.push({ key, avg, validAt: ts, len: rec.dq.length });
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const rec = this.map.get(top.key);
      const avg = rec && rec.dq.length ? rec.sum / rec.dq.length : -Infinity;
      if (top.avg !== avg) continue; // stale entry
      res.push(top.key);
    }
    // optional: reinsert from a temp to preserve heap state
    return res;
  }
}
```

